From c27ed951344f93f0fc10fb78b4f9c3097dd70413 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: root <root@ubuntu.localdomain>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 03:06:21 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 07/10] feat(rlharness): per-frame readback with timing, closes
 the WP2 readback row

AfterDrawFrame() runs between EndScene and Present, where the back buffer still
holds this frame, and glReadPixels is timed with CLOCK_MONOTONIC.  READBACK <0|1>
toggles it and reports the accumulated frames and microseconds, so the cost can
be differenced inside one process instead of inferred from wall clock (which
would also include the Python round trip).

Measured on Stage 1 Normal, 300 frames each way in the same process:

  readback off: 145.6 fps
  readback on : 130.4 fps   (-10.4%)
  glReadPixels: 5,329.5 us/frame

The 5.3 ms of readback only costs 10.4% of throughput because it forces the same
synchronisation Present would wait for anyway; it is not additive with the frame
time.  Still worth replacing with a PBO double buffer later, because a 5.3 ms
synchronous stall limits how much a single env instance can overlap.

Spread over the 16-instance aggregate measured in EXP-P0-003, this puts the
render-plus-readback total at roughly 1,080 fps, i.e. still inside the 500-2,000
band of the WP2 decision table: continue, with P3/P4 budgeted from measurements.
